.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
.Dd November 13, 2026
.Dt NQDSTATS 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqdstats ,
.Nm nqddrift ,
.Nm nqddcal
.Nd summarise a CSV file, compare a later one with the summary, and calibrate it
.Sh LIBRARY
libnqd, \-lnqd \-lnoqtis
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In nqd.h
.Bd -literal
typedef struct {
  double psiwarn; /* 0.1 */
  double psifail; /* 0.25 */
  double alpha;   /* 0.001 */
  double minn;    /* 1000 */
  int benford;    /* 0 */
} nqddopt;
.Ed
.Ft int
.Fo nqdstats
.Fa "const char *dir" "const char *path" "const nqdcsvopt *o"
.Fa "char *out" "size_t cap" "size_t *outlen" "char *why" "size_t wcap"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqddrift
.Fa "const char *stats" "size_t n" "const char *file"
.Fa "const nqdcsvopt *o" "const nqddopt *d" "nqdrep **rep"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqddcal
.Fa "const char *stats" "size_t n" "const char *const *files" "size_t nf"
.Fa "const nqdcsvopt *o" "char *out" "size_t cap" "size_t *outlen"
.Fa "char *why" "size_t wcap"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn nqdstats
reads the CSV file
.Fa path
in
.Fa dir
and writes its summaries to
.Fa out
as a
.Ql stats
element in canonical JSON, for
.Xr nqdput 3 ;
.Fa o
is as for
.Xr nqdinfer 3
(its
.Fa ranges
unused), and may be
.Dv NULL .
.Pp
.Fn nqddrift
compares the CSV file at
.Fa file
(any path) with the
.Ql stats
element
.Fa stats
.Pf ( Fa n
bytes): one
.Ql drift
row for the row counts, and per column a
.Ql drift
row (passed or failed) or a
.Ql drift.warn
row (passed, with a warning).
Each column's row carries its numbers as JSON, read with
.Xr nqdrepj 3 .
.Fa d
sets the levels
.Pf ( Dv NULL :
the defaults above).
.Xr noqtis-drift 1
defines the summaries, the tests, the levels and the error of the
sketch.
.Pp
.Fn nqddcal
calibrates the warnings of the
.Ql stats
element
.Fa stats
.Pf ( Fa n
bytes) on the
.Fa nf
CSV files
.Fa files ,
known not to have drifted from it: each is compared with it as
.Fn nqddrift
does with the default levels, and every column gets a
.Cm cal
object: the number of files
.Pq Cm n ,
the largest PSI
.Pq Cm psi ,
the smallest KS or chi-square p-value
.Pq Cm p
and, for a numeric column, the smallest Benford p-value
.Pq Cm bp .
The element with them is written to
.Fa out
as
.Fn nqdstats
writes one.
.Fn nqddrift
then warns of a calibrated column only for a PSI above
.Cm psi
or a p-value below
.Cm p
.Pq Cm bp No for Benford ,
besides the levels of
.Fa d ;
when a column fails does not change.
.Pp
All three hold every number of a column in memory while reading a
file, 8 bytes each.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn nqdstats
returns
.Dv NQOK ,
.Dv NQEBUF
with
.Fa *outlen
set when
.Fa cap
is too small,
.Dv NQEDEC
for a file that is not CSV or has rows of different lengths,
.Dv NQEIO
for a file that cannot be read,
.Dv NQELIM
over the reader's limits.
.Fn nqddrift
returns
.Dv NQOK
when no row failed and
.Dv NQEVRFY
when one did (an element that is not
.Ql stats ,
or a file that cannot be read, is a failed row), with
.Fa *rep
set.
.Fn nqddcal
returns
.Dv NQOK ,
.Dv NQEBUF
with
.Fa *outlen
set when
.Fa cap
is too small,
.Dv NQEDEC
with a sentence in
.Fa why
for an element that is not
.Ql stats
or a file that lacks one of its columns, cannot be read or has values
of another kind,
.Dv NQEARG
for no files.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis-drift 1 ,
.Xr nqdchk 3 ,
.Xr nqdinfer 3 ,
.Xr noqtis-data 5
